title Caravan phases -- a turn cut into phases keeps an order its regions never name
prompt Autonomous loop -- take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first on the Microkernel Target double-seat, Standfast on reds first, one council-rota doc deep-read, send the increment
think Standfast first. Walked crux/REDS.md end to end -- rows 80 through 87 all carry a closure or a released accounting -- 80 booked the count loom that 81's closure proved GREEN, 82 closed the same round with the boundary residue planted in its own selftest, 84 closed 20260819.145920, 85 reopened and closed 20260819.134229, 86 released. No open agent-closable red stands, so the line was clear for constructive work.
think Running thread from the top three log recommends. All three name one arc -- overtake derived the lawful order from regions, commute read the permission beside each region, intent let an item declare the act of its own hop. The newest recommend named the crux exactly -- the letters describe one whole hop, so an item that reads a region for most of its turn and writes it once at the end declares w and orders every reader behind it, and the question is whether a turn may be cut into phases a supervisor can schedule between without the declaration growing a second dialect.
think Read the mechanism before designing against it. A capability is handed at spawn and a child is spawned once, so a supervisor cannot re-hand a line mid-turn -- the Microkit shape has no move there. Read gap.rye's verbs and found pass indivisible -- it hears a bell, walks the inbound head, and writes onward as one act, so splitting a verb was never on the table. That closed the naive reading of the crux honestly rather than by assertion.
think The honest cut is one an author already has -- write the turn as smaller items, each naming one hop. Then the real question moved: is cutting SAFE? Walked overtake.share_a_hop and found the answer plainly no. Two phases of one turn that share no region read as two unrelated items, nothing orders them, and choose_pass may admit the second while the first still waits. The author wrote the order and the regions never carried it. That is the finding, and it was latent in every rung before this one.
think Designed the link as a fact about the queue rather than the document. Phase carries turn and step beside the Task, so every rung before this reads the same Task it always read; items_contend adds exactly one clause -- same turn means ordered -- and delegates everything else to intent.items_contend unchanged. The declaration grows no second dialect, which was the crux's own condition.
think Predicted the lap before building it. For a phase to run out of turn the earlier phase must fail may_pass where the later one passes, so I needed an item between head and the cut turn that contends with the first phase and not the second. serial_virt peek ask_b does exactly that against client_b place ask_b (w) and client_b peek menu (r). Predicted 1 out-of-turn by act and 0 linked, 1 pass against 0. Both landed exactly on the first metal pass.
think The concurrency half needed nothing and the cut is why -- two phases of one turn share a domain, and commute.admissible already refuses to stand a domain beside itself. Asked the isolation room honestly and left it where intent.rye left it rather than converting it for symmetry.
obs Council rota lap 471 mod 27 = 12 -- growing-a-language, read deep. Its fourth move, diverge by strengthening rather than rewriting, shaped the whole round: phases.rye adds one clause to one function and delegates the rest to intent.rye unconverted, so a difference in the report is a difference in exactly one fact. Its first move -- stand on proven ground -- is why the cut rides beside the Task rather than inside it.
obs The cost is named rather than hidden. A link is a constraint, so it can only take passes away -- 1 by act against 0 linked, and a peak of 2 against 1. Safety first, then the slot, in TAME order. A rung that reported only the gain would have been dishonest here, because there is no gain: the whole value is that the order the author cut is kept.
obs Both RED paths proven on metal before GREEN was claimed. Removing the link from items_contend reds the cut read straight at two phases of one turn reading as free to swap. Hiding the link from the choosing alone fires the linked run's own fill_table invariant on metal -- reached unreachable at phases.rye.zig:407 -- which proves that assert load-bearing rather than decorative.
obs plan_is_ordered reads the cut once at the door rather than trusting it forever. The link keeps whatever order the queue holds, so a plan listing a turn's steps shuffled would be kept faithfully in the wrong order; refusing it at the door is the cheaper honesty.
loom witness=caravan_phases_witness lap=cut_queue phases=5 out_of_turn_by_act=1 out_of_turn_linked=0 passes_by_act=1 passes_linked=0 peak_by_act=2 peak_linked=1 reaped=5
file caravan/phases.rye the rung -- Phase, same_turn, plan_is_ordered, items_contend with the link, and a supervisor that counts what it admits out of turn
file tools/caravan_phases_witness.rish the witness -- GREEN on metal, native contains checks, both RED paths named in its head
file caravan/README.md the roster row and the Phases section, docs synced in the same commit as the code
file crux/REMEMBER.md the live edge promoted to phases, the intent and commute edges folded into the prior-edges pointer, git nib refreshed in the work commit
recommend keep-going the honest next crux is what the link still cannot express -- it orders a turn's phases totally, in the sequence they were written, which is stronger than most turns need, and a turn whose middle two phases are genuinely independent pays for an order it never asked for; the question becomes whether a phase may name the phases it depends on rather than merely the turn it belongs to, without the queue growing a graph a supervisor has to walk
